  "query_preview": "..\n\nA weak cold front will progress across SE MI this afternoon and \nprovide the primary focus for isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorm development. Around 1500 J/kg of SBCAPE will be \navailable ahead and along of the boundary although deep-layer shear \nremains on the weaker side at 20 knots or less. This will favor \npulse-type convection with limited organization. Some better \ncoverage will be possible within the Thumb, aided by localized \nconvergence near the land/lake interface. The severe weather threat \nremains very low, but steep-low level lapse rates and elevated DCAPE \nvalues will support the low-end risk for a localized strong wet \nmicroburst with any robust development. Shower and thunderstorm \nchances end after the passage of the front this evening. \n\nForecast confidence decreases considerably tomorrow into Monday as \nattention turns to convective development upstream across the Upper \nMidwest. Strengthening low-level south flow will bring enhanced \nmoisture transport across Iowa and Minnesota, supporting \nthunderstorms along the nose of the LLJ. Propagation of the \nforecasted MCS east or southeast will place the Great Lakes region \nat risk for shower and thunderstorm potential by later in the day on \nSunday. Surface-based instability in this setup looks limited \nlocally, especially by the later evening hours, although building\nelevated instability will be capable of sustaining thunderstorm \npotential. Elevated activity would also bring potential for locally \nheavy rainfall and hail. \n\nEvolution of storms leading into Monday will then be highly \ndependent on Sunday activity as outflow boundaries will have \nsubstantial influence on Monday instability and subsequent storm \ndevelopment. Should any outflow settle south of the state line, \nhigher stability over Michigan affords much higher probabilities for  \nmore limited storm development. Where conversely a lingering \nboundary across the cwa could be a focal point for multiple rounds of\nrenewed thunderstorm development, potentially strong given the better\nshear, along with localized heavy rainfall with any thunderstorm \ntraining.",
